Quick recall, a database refers to the physical storage.
we don't read or write from the disks. 
So the database holds active data in the RAM or buffer
so the data in the buffer is there, but not yet locked into permanent history on to the disk

the architecture is like this
python code -> sql session -> db buffer/RAM->hard disk

so when creating an async_session
flush means data moves from code to buffer

commit means it's written into the disk

so the database.py file, accoridng to the separation of concerns, handles only connections to the db
along with session creation
the get_db function for instance creates an asyncsession to the db, and awaits for a commit, before the connection is closed
